{"at": "2026-09-26T09:04:17Z", "by": {"agent": {"model": "claude-haiku-4-5", "tool": "Claude Code"}, "identity": {"id": "RemyDegenne", "kind": "github"}, "involvement": "outsider", "kind": "agent"}, "checked": {"F1": "checked", "F2": "checked", "F3": "unchecked", "F4": "unchecked", "F5": "checked", "F6": "unchecked", "F7": "checked", "F9": "checked", "naming": "checked"}, "id": "c770471293608cc8", "kind": "review", "origin": {"kind": "issue", "ref": "LeanTrustBuilders/review-sandbox#1"}, "rationale": "The definition ∀ n, ∃ m, n < m ∧ P m correctly captures infinitely many: for any bound, there exists a larger number satisfying P.", "reference": {"text": "standard constructive definition of infinitely many"}, "schema": "ltb-evidence/0", "subject": {"commit": "c045fa30020c79c88142c589b97ae5158ff2a3ff", "hasher": {"local": "ltb-local-v1", "name": "semantic_hash", "revision": "0496f6d7b650cb03c9ffc61089ffd400dfd98564"}, "hashes": {"content": "037d254d41e594d6", "local": "6ac95b46b9195fe1", "meaning": "037d254d41e594d6"}, "kind": "definition", "module": "Sandbox.Euclid", "name": "Sandbox.InfinitelyMany", "package": "Sandbox", "toolchain": "leanprover/lean4:v4.35.0-rc2"}, "verdict": "accept"}
{"at": "2026-09-26T09:04:32Z", "by": {"agent": {"model": "claude-haiku-4-5", "tool": "Claude Code"}, "identity": {"id": "RemyDegenne", "kind": "github"}, "involvement": "outsider", "kind": "agent"}, "checked": {"F1": "checked", "F2": "checked", "F3": "unchecked", "F4": "unchecked", "F5": "unchecked", "F6": "unchecked", "F7": "unchecked", "F9": "unchecked", "naming": "checked"}, "id": "88bf1b8c4f5bb91e", "kind": "review", "origin": {"kind": "issue", "ref": "LeanTrustBuilders/review-sandbox#2"}, "rationale": "Statement correctly expresses Euclid's theorem: InfinitelyMany IsPrime means for any bound, there is a larger prime number.", "reference": {"text": "Euclid's theorem statement"}, "schema": "ltb-evidence/0", "subject": {"commit": "c045fa30020c79c88142c589b97ae5158ff2a3ff", "hasher": {"local": "ltb-local-v1", "name": "semantic_hash", "revision": "0496f6d7b650cb03c9ffc61089ffd400dfd98564"}, "hashes": {"content": "a405e2f6a57adb87", "local": "7a481dc73249882c", "meaning": "1597c9d5312718d8"}, "kind": "statement", "module": "Sandbox.Euclid", "name": "Sandbox.infinitely_many_primes", "package": "Sandbox", "toolchain": "leanprover/lean4:v4.35.0-rc2"}, "verdict": "accept"}
{"at": "2026-09-26T09:06:55Z", "by": {"agent": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5", "tool": "Claude Code"}, "identity": {"id": "RemyDegenne", "kind": "github"}, "involvement": "outsider", "kind": "agent"}, "checked": {"F1": "checked", "F2": "checked", "F3": "checked", "F4": "checked", "F5": "unchecked", "F6": "checked", "F7": "checked", "F9": "unchecked", "naming": "checked"}, "id": "13ce6ce9475f0c4f", "kind": "review", "origin": {"kind": "issue", "ref": "LeanTrustBuilders/review-sandbox#3"}, "rationale": "Divides d n := ∃ k, n = d * k is definitionally equal to Nat's ∣ (divides_iff_dvd proved by Iff.rfl), so it inherits the standard edge-case conventions for free: every d divides 0, 0 divides only 0, and 1 divides everything.", "reference": {"text": "Lean core's Dvd instance for Nat (a ∣ b := ∃ c, b = a * c); the file's own theorem divides_iff_dvd : Divides d n ↔ d ∣ n is proved by Iff.rfl, so Divides is definitionally the same relation as Nat's ∣."}, "schema": "ltb-evidence/0", "subject": {"commit": "c045fa30020c79c88142c589b97ae5158ff2a3ff", "hasher": {"local": "ltb-local-v1", "name": "semantic_hash", "revision": "0496f6d7b650cb03c9ffc61089ffd400dfd98564"}, "hashes": {"content": "e03a0bf654de900f", "local": "f19f2c59cdf612c1", "meaning": "e03a0bf654de900f"}, "kind": "definition", "module": "Sandbox.Divides", "name": "Sandbox.Divides", "package": "Sandbox", "toolchain": "leanprover/lean4:v4.35.0-rc2"}, "verdict": "accept"}
{"at": "2026-09-26T09:07:09Z", "by": {"agent": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5", "tool": "Claude Code"}, "identity": {"id": "RemyDegenne", "kind": "github"}, "involvement": "outsider", "kind": "agent"}, "id": "9e615bb80ba3c989", "kind": "review", "origin": {"kind": "issue", "ref": "LeanTrustBuilders/review-sandbox#4"}, "problem": {"category": "F3"}, "rationale": "IsPrime 1 is provable, wrongly making 1 prime. The guard is 1 ≤ p, and the only divisor of 1 is 1 itself, so the clause 'every divisor of p is 1 or p' holds vacuously at p = 1. I confirmed 'example : IsPrime 1 := ⟨Nat.le_refl 1, fun d hd => by have := Nat.eq_one_of_dvd_one (divides_iff_dvd.mp hd); omega⟩' type-checks in the project with no errors. Standard convention excludes 1 from the primes (it requires p ≥ 2), and this file's own exists_prime_divides has to separately conjoin '2 ≤ p' alongside IsPrime p, which is exactly the guard IsPrime itself is missing.", "schema": "ltb-evidence/0", "subject": {"commit": "c045fa30020c79c88142c589b97ae5158ff2a3ff", "hasher": {"local": "ltb-local-v1", "name": "semantic_hash", "revision": "0496f6d7b650cb03c9ffc61089ffd400dfd98564"}, "hashes": {"content": "f7af490739d79ed2", "local": "bc70802a882e68b7", "meaning": "f7af490739d79ed2"}, "kind": "definition", "module": "Sandbox.Prime", "name": "Sandbox.IsPrime", "package": "Sandbox", "toolchain": "leanprover/lean4:v4.35.0-rc2"}, "verdict": "problem"}
{"at": "2026-09-26T09:10:01Z", "by": {"agent": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "tool": "Claude Code"}, "identity": {"id": "RemyDegenne", "kind": "github"}, "involvement": "unknown", "kind": "agent"}, "id": "aecaad098264f1f1", "kind": "comment", "links": {"replies_to": "9e615bb80ba3c989"}, "origin": {"kind": "comment", "ref": "https://github.com/LeanTrustBuilders/review-sandbox/issues/4#issuecomment-5844875649"}, "schema": "ltb-evidence/0", "subject": {"commit": "c045fa30020c79c88142c589b97ae5158ff2a3ff", "hasher": {"local": "ltb-local-v1", "name": "semantic_hash", "revision": "0496f6d7b650cb03c9ffc61089ffd400dfd98564"}, "hashes": {"content": "f7af490739d79ed2", "local": "bc70802a882e68b7", "meaning": "f7af490739d79ed2"}, "kind": "definition", "module": "Sandbox.Prime", "name": "Sandbox.IsPrime", "package": "Sandbox", "toolchain": "leanprover/lean4:v4.35.0-rc2"}, "text": "Confirmed: `IsPrime 1` holds with the guard `1 ≤ p`. The fix makes it `2 ≤ p`, and adds a non-example `not_isPrime_one`, so that Lean keeps checking this edge case from now on."}
{"at": "2026-09-26T09:11:17Z", "by": {"agent": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "tool": "Claude Code"}, "identity": {"id": "RemyDegenne", "kind": "github"}, "involvement": "unknown", "kind": "agent"}, "commit": "751c293594e7", "id": "9f0e30100e73ca03", "kind": "status", "note": "IsPrime now requires 2 ≤ p, and not_isPrime_one pins the edge case.", "origin": {"kind": "comment", "ref": "https://github.com/LeanTrustBuilders/review-sandbox/issues/4#issuecomment-5844885360"}, "schema": "ltb-evidence/0", "state": "fixed", "target": "9e615bb80ba3c989"}
{"at": "2026-09-26T09:10:41Z", "by": {"agent": {"model": "claude-haiku-4-5", "tool": "Claude Code"}, "identity": {"id": "RemyDegenne", "kind": "github"}, "involvement": "outsider", "kind": "agent"}, "id": "cc8e8d9673f28fe0", "kind": "review", "origin": {"kind": "issue", "ref": "LeanTrustBuilders/review-sandbox#5"}, "rationale": "The definition uses ∃ k, n = d * k with unconstrained k. Does the docstring's claim that '0 divides only 0' follow from the fact that 0 * k = 0 for any k, or is there an intended constraint on k that the definition doesn't make explicit?", "schema": "ltb-evidence/0", "subject": {"commit": "751c293594e730f4a22a00f6c0ae3bf95ca674d8", "hasher": {"local": "ltb-local-v1", "name": "semantic_hash", "revision": "0496f6d7b650cb03c9ffc61089ffd400dfd98564"}, "hashes": {"content": "e03a0bf654de900f", "local": "f19f2c59cdf612c1", "meaning": "e03a0bf654de900f"}, "kind": "definition", "module": "Sandbox.Divides", "name": "Sandbox.Divides", "package": "Sandbox", "toolchain": "leanprover/lean4:v4.35.0-rc2"}, "verdict": "question"}
